{"text":"Original [SASRec sampling](https://github.com/kang205/SASRec/blob/master/sampler.py) rejection-samples uniform item IDs against `set(user_train[user])`, excluding the complete training history, and returns user IDs with sequences. It does not enforce uniqueness across positions. The official [gSASRec PyTorch sampler](https://github.com/asash/gSASRec-pytorch/blob/main/dataset_utils.py) instead draws a B×L×K tensor with `torch.randint`: duplicates and history collisions are possible. gSASRec uses gBCE; candidate-softmax is a separate loss baseline.

Implementation recommendation: cache each user’s complete training-history set or sorted array. Carry user IDs or dataset-row indices through sampling, shuffling and collation; `(None, sequence, positive, negative)` cannot reliably identify users because truncated histories can coincide. Preserve the diagnostic tuple through an adapter while training receives explicit row metadata. For each valid position, draw uniform IDs, reject padding, history items and already accepted negatives, then refill until K unique items remain. Batched rejection avoids catalog-sized masks; dense histories can use cached complements sampled without replacement. Require K≤available unseen items. Seed sampler workers explicitly and preserve deterministic batch ordering; never consult held-out interactions.

For memory-efficient candidate-softmax, chunk valid positions while keeping all K+1 candidates within each chunk. Compute encoder features H once, create `Z=H.detach().requires_grad_(True)`, and backpropagate each chunk’s summed cross-entropy divided by the total valid-position count. Candidate-embedding gradients accumulate while each chunk graph is freed; finally call `H.backward(Z.grad)` and step once. This applies the [autograd chain rule](https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/2.14/generated/torch.autograd.backward.html), including both gradient paths for tied embeddings. Averaging chunk means misweights uneven chunks; summing loss tensors before backward retains their graphs. Splitting candidates instead requires a shared softmax normalizer."}
